## Track 2: Survey Wheel Returns at Kilometer 43

**Story & Context:** At 124 BPM, the EP’s most propulsive track uses a nine-attack Kilometer Cell against constant eighth-note hi-hat and quarter-note kick. Ieva adds tambourine only after the third phase cycle, increasing speed without adding rhythmic complexity. Matas uses Marker Exchange to move emphasis on the fourth kanklės attack from clean guitar to floor tom to organ and finally to silence. The same cell therefore appears to change shape even though Austėja never alters it.

Greta’s bass harmonica is withheld until the principal Survey Return near the two-minute mark. She plays a short fifth-to-tonic breath phrase, and Felix refuses the expected drum fill. The drums remain exactly the same, making the convergence itself the event. A late bridge removes snare and organ but keeps kick and hat, exposing Amber Ghost resonance around the kanklės body.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Brass survey wheel resting beside enamel kilometer plate 43, nine graphite ticks drawn around a circular route diagram, orange field flag reflected in rain-dark rail steel, amber and moss-green palette. Sparse lyric fragments: “forty-three,” “wheel comes back,” “same speed,” “mark the return.”

## Track 3: Last Train Never Corrects the Pattern

**Story & Context:** The closer runs at 108 BPM and begins with an eleven-attack kanklės cell over only kick and closed hi-hat. Felix does not add snare for the first thirty-two bars. Matas holds a soft add9 organ chord while Greta plays breath-only bass-harmonica noise at the ends of selected cycles. The long cell takes much more time to feel familiar, so the phase drift seems less mechanical and more like a landscape slowly changing outside a train window.

Halfway through, Ieva enters with one floor-tom stroke at each Marker Exchange while Matas adds a two-note guitar drone. No instrument follows the kanklės downbeat. In the final minute, the arrangement gradually removes layers: tambourine, guitar, snare, organ, then bass harmonica. Austėja and Felix remain alone. The kanklės pattern continues drifting until one final natural Survey Return arrives. Neither musician accents it. The EP ends two bars later, proving that convergence does not have to function as resolution.

